   - Scenario create/switch code paths copy the whole settings struct
     (StartDate included) without independently re-deriving it -> no
     drift introduced.

5. Concurrency / cache: UpdateSettingsWithPersons takes sm.mu.Lock() around
   load+shift+save (pre-existing lock, unchanged by diff).
   `loadInternal`/`loadInternalContext` always reads fresh from the file
   store (no `sm.cache` read) so the elapsed computation is always against
   the truthful on-disk StartDate, not a page-rendered/cached one. Walked
   through the "clock crosses a month boundary while a current-month plan's
   page is open" race by hand: posted (page-rendered, already-advanced)
   start_date vs loaded (stale, on-disk) start_date produces exactly the
   elapsed the clock would have produced anyway; resolveCurrentMonth then
   sees elapsed==0 against the just-set StartDate and does not double-shift.
   This mechanism (lock + saveInternal->resolveCurrentMonth ordering) is
   pre-existing and shared by every other settings mutation, not
   introduced or altered by this diff.

6. Round-trip (move later then back earlier): shiftRange is a plain
   subtraction with only a `max(0, ...)` floor; for a non-clamped item
   forward-then-backward is an exact algebraic inverse. Only items that
   got clamped to 0 lose data on the round trip, which D2 explicitly
   accepts ("kept, never charged" / "already running" — the clamp loss is
   by design, not a defect).

7. Field coverage: shiftScheduleOffsets shifts IncomeSources +
   RemovedIncomeSources, ExpenseSources + RemovedExpenseSources,
   OneTimeExpenses (no Removed variant exists in the model), BigTicketItems
   + RemovedBigTicketItems. `grep StartMonth\|EndMonth` across
   internal/models turns up no other persisted schedule-offset field left
   unshifted. Roth conversion (`StartYear`/`EndYear`, year-granular), ages,
   and SS are untouched by shiftScheduleOffsets, matching D2's explicit
   exclusion list. onetime-card.html / bigticket-card.html render the
   calendar-month label and "(past)" flag unconditionally (no
   distance-based hiding), so a far shift cannot silently drop an item
   from the rendered surface the oracle checks.

8. Rendering surfaces: the oracle's probe.js reads the actual DOM (input
   values for income/expense start/end, button-adjacent text labels for
   one-time/big-ticket), not just the saved JSON — this covers the
   split-classification concern (enumerate every surface, not just the
   diff) for this task's figures.

